--TEST--
A `covariant` property is tested on every write path that names no class the compiler can check — a write through `object` and through `mixed`, a `property<T>` key, `Core\Reflect`, and a hooked property before its guard, its `set` hook and its observer, and again where the parent's hook stores another value — and a value of the wider type throws the `LogicError` a typed write throws, while a value no declaration takes still throws the `RuntimeError` it always did — `rule:classes/a-covariant-type-is-checked-where-a-value-enters`
--FILE--
<?nvs
class Animal {}
class Dog extends Animal {}
class Cat extends Animal {}

class Home {
    public Animal $pet;
    public ?Animal $guest = null;
    public function constructor(Animal $pet) { $this->pet = $pet; }
}

class DogHome extends Home {
    public covariant Dog $pet;
    public Animal|null $guest = null;
    public function constructor(Animal $pet) { parent::constructor($pet); }
}

class Pen implements PropertyGuard, PropertyObserver {
    public Animal $pet {
        set (Animal $v) {
            echo "  Pen set\n";
            $this->pet = $v;
        }
    }
    public function constructor(Animal $pet) { $this->pet = $pet; }
    public function beforePropertySet(string $name, mixed $value, array<string> $keys): void {
        echo "  guard ", $name, "\n";
    }
    public function beforePropertyUnset(string $name, array<string> $keys): void {}
    public function onPropertyGet(string $name, mixed $value): void {}
    public function onPropertySet(string $name, mixed $value): void {
        echo "  observer ", $name, "\n";
    }
}

class DogPen extends Pen {
    public covariant Dog $pet {
        set (Dog $v) {
            echo "  DogPen set\n";
            $this->pet = $v;
        }
    }
    public function constructor(Animal $pet) { parent::constructor($pet); }
}

class Cot {
    public lateinit Animal $pet {
        set (Animal $v) {
            $this->pet = $v is Dog ? new Cat() : $v;
        }
    }
}

class DogCot extends Cot {
    public lateinit covariant Dog $pet;
}

DogHome $d = new DogHome(new Dog());

// 1. A write through `object`.
object $o = $d;
try {
    $o->pet = new Cat();
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo "1: ", $e->message, "\n";
}

// 2. A write through `mixed`.
mixed $m = $d;
try {
    $m->pet = new Cat();
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo "2: ", $e->message, "\n";
}

// 3. A write through a `property<Home>` key.
Home $h = $d;
property<Home> $key = "pet" as property<Home>;
try {
    $h->$key = new Cat();
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo "3: ", $e->message, "\n";
}

// 4. A write through `Core\Reflect`.
try {
    Core\Reflect::forObject($d)->set($d, "pet", new Cat());
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo "4: ", $e->message, "\n";
}

// Each path still writes a `Dog`, and the property was never a `Cat`.
$o->pet = new Dog();
$h->$key = new Dog();
echo $d->pet is Dog ? "a dog" : "not a dog", "\n";

// 5. A value that no declaration of the property takes throws a `RuntimeError`.
try {
    $o->pet = "Rex";
} catch (RuntimeError $wrong) {
    echo "5: RuntimeError\n";
}

// 6. `Animal|null` is the same type as `?Animal`, so a `Cat` is written.
$o->guest = new Cat();
echo $d->guest is Cat ? "a cat" : "not a cat", "\n";

// 7. A hooked property is tested before the guard, the hook and the observer run.
Pen $p = new DogPen(new Dog());
echo "write a Cat through Pen\n";
try {
    $p->pet = new Cat();
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo "7: ", $e->message, "\n";
}
echo "write a Cat through object\n";
object $po = $p;
try {
    $po->pet = new Cat();
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo "7: ", $e->message, "\n";
}
echo "write a Dog through object\n";
$po->pet = new Dog();

// 8. The parent's `set` hook stores a `Cat` in place of the `Dog` it was given.
Cot $cot = new DogCot();
try {
    $cot->pet = new Dog();
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo "8: ", $e->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
1: The property `$pet` of `DogHome` has the type `Dog`. The value is an object of class `Cat`.
2: The property `$pet` of `DogHome` has the type `Dog`. The value is an object of class `Cat`.
3: The property `$pet` of `DogHome` has the type `Dog`. The value is an object of class `Cat`.
4: The property `$pet` of `DogHome` has the type `Dog`. The value is an object of class `Cat`.
a dog
5: RuntimeError
a cat
  guard pet
  Pen set
  observer pet
write a Cat through Pen
7: The property `$pet` of `DogPen` has the type `Dog`. The value is an object of class `Cat`.
write a Cat through object
7: The property `$pet` of `DogPen` has the type `Dog`. The value is an object of class `Cat`.
write a Dog through object
  guard pet
  DogPen set
  observer pet
8: The property `$pet` of `DogCot` has the type `Dog`. The value is an object of class `Cat`.
